          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong tree
            is a connected
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph"} graph
            without cycles. Some notions of tree (such as planar tree) consider extra structure or properties as well, but the baseline notion is tree as acyclic connected graph.
          %p Trees are fundamental combinatorial objects which appear in a wide variety of guises. Some are given below.
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#as_undirected_graphs As undirected graphs
          %p
            Recall that an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/undirected+graph"} undirected graph
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            consists of a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            of
            %em vertices
            and a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            of unordered pairs of vertices. A
            %strong path
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a finite sequence of vertices
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0, \ldots, x_n
            such that each pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i x_{i+1}
            belongs to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_n = x_0
            = succeed "." do
              %strong cycle
            %strong connected
            if it is nonempty and if for every distinct pair of vertices
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0 = x
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_n = y
            %strong acyclic
            if the only cycles are paths
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0, \ldots, x_{n-1}, x_n, x_{n-1}, \ldots, x_0
          %p
            where steps are retraced; an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/acyclic+graph"} acyclic graph
            is also called a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forest"} forest
            %strong tree
            is a connected forest.
          %p
            Each forest is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint sum
            (a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
            in the category of graphs) of trees. Removal of a vertex of a tree (and any edges incident to it) results in a forest.
          %p
            Not all authors include nonemptiness as part of the notion of connectedness. See
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+space"} connected space
            for commentary on this. Forests on the other hand may be empty.
          %h3#as_spaces As spaces
          %p
            A
            %em topological tree
            is a 1-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW-complex"} CW-complex
            which is contractible, i.e., homotopy equivalent to a point.
          %p
            In this language, a topological graph is simply a 1-dimensional CW-complex; more precisely, we mean a
            %em CW-presentation
            and not merely a space that can be so presented. A topological forest is then a 1-dimensional CW-complex with vanishing first
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology+group"} homology group
            with integral coefficients, and a tree is a connected forest.
          %p
            For finite graphs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            as CW-complexes, one has the useful
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler+characteristic"} Euler formula
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mn 0
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 0
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_0 - h_1 = c_0 - c_1
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_i
            is the number of
            = succeed "-cells" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_i
            is the rank of the homology group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo ;
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_i(G; \mathbb{Z})
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_0 = 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_0
            be 1 greater than the number of edges
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1
          %h3#as_digraphs As digraphs
          %p
            A
            %strong rooted tree
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+graph"} directed graph
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} quiver
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            such that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+category+on+a+quiver"} free category
            generated by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            has a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            %strong root
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            there is exactly one directed path from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            to the root. It follows easily that the free category is a poset. There is no significant change in content if “terminal” here is replaced by “initial”; we can distinguish the conventions by a tree directed
            %em towards
            the root rather than
            %em away from
            the root.)
          %p
            A
            %strong rooted forest
            is a coproduct of rooted trees, and again we have the fundamental fact that there is a natural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tree
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mo →
                  %mi Forest
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tree \overset{\to}{\leftarrow} Forest
          %p which in one direction is the functor which sends a tree to the forest which results by excising the root and all edges incident to it, and in the other sends a forest to the tree gotten by adjoining a new root and joining the roots of the forest to that new root by new edges.
          %p
            This equivalence can be exploited to relate the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+generating+function"} exponential generating function
            for finite rooted trees to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lambert+W-function"} Lambert W-function
            (q.v.).
          %p
            A tree in the form of an
            %em undirected
            graph together with a chosen vertex can be regarded as a rooted tree in digraph form (with root the chosen vertex), by orienting all edges in the direction of paths going to the root. The category of rooted trees has very nice categorical properties not shared by the category of unrooted trees; see the following section.
          %h3#as_ordered_sets As ordered sets
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order+theory"} order theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} partial order
            with the added property that it is downward
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well+ordering"} well ordered
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            be a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partially+ordered+set"} partially ordered set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is a tree when for each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in S
            the downward closure or history
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mo −
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mi y
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^- = \{ y \mid y \leq x \}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well+ordered"} well ordered
            subset of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^-
            is only totally ordered (meaning that points in the tree do not have a distinguished successor) we say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prefix+ordered"} prefix ordered
          %h3#AsFunctors As functors
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/totally+ordered+set"} totally ordered set
            of natural numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi …
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq 1 \leq 2 \leq \ldots
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %strong rooted forest
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: \mathbb{N}^{op} \to Set
          %p
            to “the”
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
            %strong rooted tree
            is a forest for which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(0)
            is terminal (a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
          %p
            In this case, we get from a tree as functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            to a tree as digraph by passing to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+elements"} category of elements
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi El
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} El(F)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi El
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} El(F)
            which map to morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i + 1 \to i
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^{op}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^{op}
            is itself (the free category on) a tree as digraph, one that is terminal in the category of forests.
          %p
            In the other direction, starting with a tree as digraph, we define a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon \mathbb{N}^{op} \to Set
            by letting
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(n)
            be the set of vertices
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            such that the path from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            to the root has
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            edges. The map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(n+1) \to F(n)
            moves a vertex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in F(n+1)
            to the vertex that is one step closer to the root (along the unique path from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            to the root).
          %p
            The functor description makes it clear that the category of forests is a (presheaf)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+trees"} category of trees
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
          %p
            If we replace
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            here by the category of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/totally+ordered+set"} totally ordered set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Tos
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tos
            %strong planar forest
            as a functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Tos
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: \mathbb{N}^{op} \to Tos
          %h3#as_terminal_coalgebra As terminal coalgebra
          %p
            The category of trees as described in the section immediately above can be described universally in 2-universal terms, as the 2-terminal coalgebra for the endofunctor on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            (locally small categories) which takes a category to its small-coproduct cocompletion. See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+coalgebra"} terminal coalgebra
            for an extended discussion.
          %h3#as_operations As operations
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{F}
            denote the free (strict) monoidal category generated by
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi θ
                      %mi n
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_n: x^n \to x
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 0
            %strong
              planar forest with (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              ) live leaves
            is a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: x^n \to x^m
            in
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{F}
            %strong
              planar tree with (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              ) live leaves
            is a morphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: x^n \to x
          %p The previous description is more a theorem than definition, but it points to the recursive construction of trees based on the fundamental equivalence between forests and trees:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The identity
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo →
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \to x
                corresponds to a root considered as a live leaf;
            %li
              %p
                Given trees with live leaves
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mi j
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi n
                              %mi j
                        %mo →
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_j \colon x^{n_j} \to x
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                        %mo ∈
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mn 1
                        %mi …
                        %mo ,
                        %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j \in \{1 \ldots, k\}
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi …
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi k
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi n
                            %mn 1
                          %mo +
                          %mi …
                          %mo +
                          %msub
                            %mi n
                            %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi n
                            %mn 1
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi …
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi n
                            %mi k
                      %mo →
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi …
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1 \otimes \ldots \otimes f_k \colon x^{n_1 + \ldots + n_k} = x^{n_1} \otimes \ldots \otimes x^{n_k} \to x \otimes \ldots \otimes x = x^k
              %p
                whose set of live leaves is the disjoint union of sets of live leaves of the
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mi j
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_j
            %li
              %p Given a forest with live leaves
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi …
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi k
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi n
                            %mn 1
                          %mo +
                          %mi …
                          %mo +
                          %msub
                            %mi n
                            %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi n
                            %mn 1
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi …
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi n
                            %mi k
                      %mo →
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi …
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1 \otimes \ldots \otimes f_k \colon x^{n_1 + \ldots + n_k} = x^{n_1} \otimes \ldots \otimes x^{n_k} \to x \otimes \ldots \otimes x = x^k
              %p there is a tree
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi θ
                        %mi n
                      %mo ∘
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi …
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi n
                            %mn 1
                          %mo +
                          %mi …
                          %mo +
                          %msub
                            %mi n
                            %mi k
                      %mo →
                      %mi x
