              %p with the same set of live leaves.
          %p
            In more straightforward combinatorial language: if a finite planar tree is a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon [n]^{op} \to \Delta_a
            from a finite ordinal to the augmented
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
            %strong leaf
            is an element without predecessors in the poset of elements of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            = succeed "." do
              %strong live
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi θ
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi I
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_0 \colon I = x^0 \to x
          %p
            Fancier versions of this basic recursive construction may involve “colored trees”, “cherry trees”, and so on, and figure prominently in the theory of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operads"} operads
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+operads"} free operads
          %p
            In fact, this type of recursivity is at the root (pun perhaps intended) of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cumulative+hierarchy"} cumulative hierarchy
            conception of sets in a material set theory like
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC set theory
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x_1, \ldots, x_n\}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+foundation"} axiom of foundation
            %em well-founded
            trees. A tree (pictured as a free category on a digraph, i.e., as a certain poset
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %strong well-founded
            if every object of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P^{op}
            is bounded above by a maximal element, or equivalently if there are no infinite chains
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo &lt;
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi …
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0 \lt x_1 \lt \ldots
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P^{op}
            starting from the root
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0
            = succeed ".)" do
              %strong branches
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-founded+relation"} well-founded relation
            for other formulations of this definition (including those suitable for
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+set"} pure set
            for details on this construction of the cumulative hierarchy, including using arbitrary trees for
            %em ill-founded
            sets.
          %p
            The category of well-founded trees admits a (2-)universal description, as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+algebra"} initial algebra
            for a certain endofunctor on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %span.newWikiWord
              well-founded forest
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/well-founded+forest"} ?
            for details.
          %p [To be continued]
          .query
            %p Very nice! Is there a similar story worth telling for possibly infinite trees, corecursion, etc.
            %p
              %em> Toby
              \: Well-founded trees (and pure sets) may be defined recursively; ill-founded trees (and pure sets) may be defined corecursively; there is stuff about this at
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+set"} pure set
          %h3#as_relational_structures As relational structures
          %p
            A
            %em rooted tree
